      "text": "This problem requires understanding of basic pyramid surface area formula and geometry, specifically the Pythagorean theorem and the area of a triangle. \n\nFirst, let's find the slant height of the pyramid. \n\nUsing the Pythagorean theorem, we can form a right triangle with the height of the pyramid (12 cm), half the length of the base (5 cm), and the slant height. Let's call the slant height \\( l \\).\n\\[\nl = \\sqrt{12^2 + 5^2} = \\sqrt{144 + 25} = \\sqrt{169} = 13 \\text{ cm}\n\\]\n\nNext, we find the area of one of the triangular faces of the pyramid. Each triangular face is a right triangle with a base of 10 cm and a height (the slant height) of 13 cm.\n\\[\n\\text{Area of one triangular face} = \\frac{1}{2} \\times \\text{base} \\times \\text{height} = \\frac{1}{2} \\times 10 \\times 13 = 65 \\text{ cm}^2\n\\]\n\nSince the pyramid has four identical triangular faces, the total area of the four triangular faces is:\n\\[\n4 \\times 65 = 260 \\text{ cm}^2\n\\]\n\nThe base of the pyramid is a square with a side length of 10 cm. Therefore, the area of the square base is:\n\\[\n10 \\times 10 = 100 \\text{ cm}^2\n\\]\n\nThe total surface area of the pyramid is the sum of the areas of the four triangular faces and the base:\n\\[\n260 + 100 = 360 \\text{ cm}^2\n\\]\n\nTherefore, the total surface area of the pyramid is \\(\\boxed{360}\\) square centimeters.",
